This paper investigates the normalized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints and growth conditions for $\beta$, $\lambda$, and $f$, and we leverage a homotopy-based minimax principle to establish the existence of a normalized ground state solution for this equation.

Recent analyses of lepton flavor universality (LFU) violating decays have suggested that new physics beyond the Standard Model might exist, potentially influencing multiple areas. To gain insight into these potential effects, we have explored their implications on the decay $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ within the context of the $U_1$ leptoquark model. By linking the new physics interactions in $b 	o c 	au  ar{
u}$ transitions to the known $b 	o c 	au  ar{
u}$ couplings, we have determined how much the former can impact the latter. Utilizing the obtained new physics parameter space by performing a comprehensive LFU analysis of $b 	o c 	au 
u$ decays, we have derived the allowed region for this process. Additionally, by integrating heavy quark effective theory (HQET) and assuming the relevant leptoquark coupling pattern, we have obtained several key observables for $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ decays, which can be verified by experimental findings from Belle II and CBM.
